Q: Is it possible to replace table cells with other elements using display: table-cell? A: <p>&quot;I have tried to make <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> cover every cell.&quot;<br />
&quot;<code>height: 100%</code> does not work unless parent element has a fixed height&quot;</p>
<p>Something like this?</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>table { height:fit-content; }
a { display:block; height:100%; }

tr { height:100%; }

td { border:1px solid black; }
a:hover { background-color:#7ff; }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;table&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=#1&gt;r1c1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=#2&gt;row 1 column 2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=#3&gt;r1&lt;br/&gt;c3&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=#4&gt;row 2 column 1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=#5&gt;r2&lt;br/&gt;c2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=#6&gt;row 2 column 3&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>I don't know why setting <code>height</code> on <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> (or <code>&lt;td&gt;</code>) is necessary inside this snippet - it doesn't seem to be needed on a plain page.</p>

Q: Query a Database for a subcategory based on the URL path when subcategories at different levels might have the same name A: <h3>Solving for matching URL paths and Categories</h3>
<p>Following this structure for what you have as categories in your DB:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My DB structure at the moment has categories with a <code>parent</code> field, so like <code>name, description, image, parentId</code>. The top level categories would have a <code>parentId</code> of null.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have created two category trees. One for <code>/category/clothes/mens/brand-a/t-shirts</code> and another for <code>/category/offers/clothes/mens/brand-a/t-shirts</code>. Both are for <code>t-shirts</code> but under different parents and paths (<code>clothes</code> vs <code>offers/clothes</code>). Also noting that <code>brand-a</code>, <code>mens</code>, <code>t-shirts</code>, etc. are not unique category names - but have different <code>_id</code>'s &amp;  <code>parentId</code>'s. I have done this based on:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As you can see in this example there is multiple categories with the same name and these can be nested at different levels with no set number of levels.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can do this with <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/graphLookup/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>$graphLookup</code></a> and starting from the <strong><em>child</em></strong> - since each child only has one parent.
Steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>match on category name for the last part; like <code>t-shirts</code> in both URLs.</li>
<li>do a graphLookup by going <em>up</em> the tree until the root/top-parent.</li>
<li>sort the child tree by their <em>reverse</em> depth
<ul>
<li>reverse is needed because we started at the child, so the child is depth 0 and the root/parent is depth 3 (for example).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>create a <code>categoryPath</code> by concatenating each name and a forward slash  <code>/</code>.
<ul>
<li>You can decide if you want to start with <code>/category</code> or not. Since that's the endpoint and not a &quot;category name&quot;, I have excluded it.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>then match the generated <code>categoryPath</code> with the URL of the request - that will give you the exact category being referenced, even if the name is not unique.</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>db.categories.aggregate([
  {
    // start with the LAST part of the URL, the child-most node
    $match: { name: &quot;t-shirts&quot; }
  },
  {
    // find parents for all matched nodes
    $graphLookup: {
      from: &quot;categories&quot;,
      startWith: &quot;$_id&quot;,
      connectFromField: &quot;parentId&quot;,
      connectToField: &quot;_id&quot;,
      as: &quot;categoryTree&quot;,
      depthField: &quot;categoryDepth&quot;
    }
  },
  {
    $set: {
      categoryTree: {
        $sortArray: {
          input: &quot;$categoryTree&quot;,
          // 'root' wanted first, &quot;deepest&quot; from the 'child'
          sortBy: { categoryDepth: -1 }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    $set: {
      categoryPath: {
        // create what the URL path for this category would be
        // without the initial endpoint `/category` name (or add it everywhere)
        $reduce: {
          input: &quot;$categoryTree&quot;,
          initialValue: &quot;&quot;,
          in: { $concat: [&quot;$$value&quot;, &quot;/&quot;, &quot;$$this.name&quot;] }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  {
    // match to the child you want and not the others under different parents
    $match: {
      // this should be the URL with all path elems
      // (except the starting `/category` of the endpoint)
      categoryPath: &quot;/offers/clothes/mens/brand-a/t-shirts&quot;
    }
  },
  { $project: { categoryTree: 0 } }
])
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://mongoplayground.net/p/4xOIyWSnz-6" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Mongo Playground with t-shirts</a> (use the stages dropdown to see what the output is before the final <code>$match</code>). Note that this is just one query to get the result; rather than 10 queries for 10 nested category searches.</p>
<p>This will also work, for top-level parents, <a href="https://mongoplayground.net/p/6ovw7UgqEy_" rel="nofollow noreferrer">like clothes</a>.</p>
<h4>Using that with your URL/endpoint</h4>
<p>Add a new route with <a href="https://reactrouter.com/api/hooks/useParams#catchall-params" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Catchall params</a> so that it's easier to deconstruct the categories in the URL path. So the routes would be:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// existing route for the current behaviour with one category id
&lt;Route path=&quot;/category/:c&quot; element={&lt;CategoryPage /&gt;} /&gt;
// add one more route for the nested category names. note the star
&lt;Route path=&quot;/category/*&quot; element={&lt;NestedCategoryPage /&gt;} /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and then <code>NestedCategoryPage</code> will have something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function NestedCategoryPage() {
  const { &quot;*&quot;: splat } = useParams();     // &quot;/offers/clothes/mens/brand-a/t-shirts&quot;
  const pathSegments = splat.split('/');  // [&quot;offers&quot;, &quot;clothes&quot;, &quot;mens&quot;, &quot;brand-a&quot;, &quot;t-shirts&quot;]
  const lastSeg = pathSegments.at(-1);    // &quot;t-shirts&quot;
  // run the aggregation with `lastSeg` and `splat`
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then use the aggregation pipeline above with <code>lastSeg</code> in the first stage match. The URL match stage towards the end should be with <code>splat</code>. Remember to remove trailing slashes in <code>splat</code> and clean consecutive slashes beforehand. And you may need to URL-decode the segments if you have categories with spaces etc.</p>
<p>You will not be able to have categories with a forward slash in the name like <code>m/w</code> since <code>/clothes/m/w/t-shirts</code> would become <code>[&quot;clothes&quot;, &quot;m&quot;, &quot;w&quot;, &quot;t-shirts&quot;]</code> instead of <code>[&quot;clothes&quot;, &quot;m/w&quot;, &quot;t-shirts&quot;]</code>; unless <code>m/f</code> is encoded as <code>m%2fw</code> in all URLs, and then you re-encode when creating <code>categoryPath</code>.</p>
<h3>Long Term solution with DB structure improvements</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>Or is there a better way to either structure my database to query for the category in question with the whole path?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, this is exactly how it should (could) be. Assuming that you want to keep arbitrarily nested categories in the URL:</p>
<ol>
<li><code>categoryPath</code> should set on every category. So use a similar aggregation pipeline to add the <code>categoryPath</code> field to all categories and update the existing collection; like this <a href="https://mongoplayground.net/p/Dm0WX4pcVmL" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mongo playground example</a>.
<ul>
<li>Also create an index on <code>categoryPath</code>.</li>
<li>So each document would be like:</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>  {
    &quot;_id&quot;: 9,
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;t-shirts&quot;,
    &quot;parentId&quot;: 8
    &quot;categoryPath&quot;: &quot;/offers/clothes/mens/brand-a/t-shirts&quot;,
    &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Men's t-shirs by brand A on special offer&quot;,
  }
</code></pre>
</li>
<li>Then use <code>findOne()</code> directly with the <code>categoryPath</code>; <a href="https://mongoplayground.net/p/Ai1soOmlYal" rel="nofollow noreferrer">usage example:</a>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>db.collection.find({
  // direct match on path
  categoryPath: &quot;/offers/clothes/mens/brand-a/t-shirts&quot;
})
</code></pre>
</li>
<li>Make sure you update all child categories' <code>categoryPath</code>'s when you add/delete/rename any parent category. Otherwise the URL &amp; <code>categoryPath</code> won't match. (Although, with just 100s of categories, regenerating them all for any change would probably be under 1 second. See <a href="https://mongoplayground.net/p/Cg_MIRhjkUA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this example with 1900 categories</a> - when run locally with 2000 categories, completes in &lt;300ms.)</li>
</ol>

Q: How to load values via System.Text.Json from a file and store them readonly? A: <p>The <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/59974756">answer</a> by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/285795/%ce%a9megaman">ΩmegaMan</a> was smart and correct when System.Text.Json was released in .NET Core 3.1, but subsequent releases have added support for <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/immutability" rel="nofollow noreferrer">immutable types</a> so you have several ways to implement your read-only <code>Settings</code> type:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Starting with <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/whats-new/dotnet-5#systemtextjson-new-features" rel="nofollow noreferrer">.NET 5</a> you can deserialize using a parameterized constructor, matching properties to constructor parameters by name.</p>
<p>If your type has exactly one public constructor, System.Text.Json will use it whether parameterized or not:</p>
<pre><code>public class Settings
{
    public Settings(decimal a, int b, double c, double d) =&gt; (A, B, C, D) = (a, b, c, d);

    public decimal A { get; private set; }
    public int B { get; private set; }
    public double C { get; private set; }
    public double D { get; private set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/builtin-types/record" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Records</a> make this particularly easy:</p>
<pre><code>public record Settings(decimal A, int B, double C, double D);
</code></pre>
<p>But if your type has multiple constructors, System.Text.Json will only select a parameterless constructor automatically so you need to mark the one to use with <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.serialization.jsonconstructorattribute" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>[JsonConstructor]</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

public class Settings
{
    [JsonConstructor] // Use this constructor when deserializing from JSON
    public Settings(decimal a, int b, double c, double d) =&gt; (A, B, C, D) = (a, b, c, d);

    public Settings() { }
    public decimal A { get; private set; }
    public int B { get; private set; }
    public double C { get; private set; }
    public double D { get; private set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you choose to use a parameterized constructor, do take note of the following important <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/immutability#parameterized-constructors" rel="nofollow noreferrer">documentation remark</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The parameter names of a parameterized constructor must match the property names and types. Matching is case-insensitive, and the constructor parameter must match the actual property name even if you use <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.text.json.serialization.jsonpropertynameattribute" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>[JsonPropertyName]</code></a> to rename a property.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Thus if you have a property  <code>decimal A {get; set;}</code> the corresponding constructor parameter must be named <code>A</code> or <code>a</code> and be of type <code>decimal</code> and not some other type like <code>string</code> or <code>double</code>.</p>
<p>Failure to follow this requirement precisely can lead to deserialization errors with cryptic messages.</p>
<p>Also, if your immutable type is a <code>struct</code>, you must <strong>always</strong> mark its parameterized constructor with <code>[JsonInclude]</code> because structs always have automatically generated default constructors.  See <em><a href="https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/94443" rel="nofollow noreferrer">JsonSerializer doesn't correctly deserialize readonly structs #94443</a></em> for confirmation.</p>
<p>Demo fiddles <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/1g80vO" rel="nofollow noreferrer">#1 here</a>, <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/2fxmU7" rel="nofollow noreferrer">#2 here</a> and <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/sPf8oS" rel="nofollow noreferrer">#3 here</a>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>System.Text.Json supports <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/keywords/init" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>init</code> properties</a> out of the box:</p>
<pre><code>public class Settings
{
    public decimal A { get; init; }
    public int B { get; init; }
    public double C { get; init; }
    public double D { get; init; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Demo <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/UPaui9" rel="nofollow noreferrer">#4 here</a>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Also starting in <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/whats-new/dotnet-5#systemtextjson-new-features" rel="nofollow noreferrer">.NET 5</a>, System.Text.Json supports private setters when marked with <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/immutability#non-public-members-and-property-accessors" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>[JsonInclude]</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

public class Settings
{
    [JsonInclude] public decimal A { get; private set; }
    [JsonInclude] public int B { get; private set; }
    [JsonInclude] public double C { get; private set; }
    [JsonInclude] public double D { get; private set; }

    // Factory methods to create settings with various inputs, e.g.
    public static Settings Create(decimal a, int b, double c, double d) =&gt; new() { A = a, B = b, C = c, D = d };
}
</code></pre>
<p>Demo #5 <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/l938K0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Finally, if you want to use dependency injection to create your <code>Settings</code> object, see maybe</p>
<ul>
<li><em><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/79753796">Polymorphic deserialize JSON using DI container to create instances</a></em></li>
<li><em><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/72853100">Is there a similar approach to CustomCreationConverter in System.Text.Json?</a></em></li>
<li><em><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/59203030">Deserialization of inteface types not supported using System.Text.JSON</a></em>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Personally I find the <code>record</code> or <code>init</code> solutions to be simplest, because they don't require applying any attributes and don't require keeping property and constructor parameter names synchronized.  <code>init</code> properties also with with <code>XmlSerializer</code>, if that matters.</p>
